        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>We know how to integrate <span class=\"math\">[MATH: e^x,]</span> but how do we integrate a function like <span class=\"math\">[MATH: e^{2x}?]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "We know how to integrate [MATH: e^x,] but how do we integrate a function like [MATH: e^{2x}?]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The answer is to use a substitution. If we let <span class=\"math\">[MATH: u=2x,]</span> then</p>",
            "readable_text": "The answer is to use a substitution. If we let [MATH: u=2x,] then",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Now, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable <span class=\"math\">[MATH: u,]</span> as follows:</p>",
            "readable_text": "Now, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable [MATH: u,] as follows:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u222be^{2x}dx|= \u222be^{u} \u22c5 (1)/(2)du; = (1)/(2)\u222be^{u}du; = (1)/(2)e^{u} + C; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} + C.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \u222be^{2x}dx|= \u222be^{u} \u22c5 (1)/(2)du; = (1)/(2)\u222be^{u}du; = (1)/(2)e^{u} + C; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} + C.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:</p>",
            "readable_text": "After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (d)/(dx)((1)/(2)e^{2x} + C)|= (1)/(2) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(e^{2x}) + (d)/(dx)(C); = (1)/(2) \u22c5 e^{2x} \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x) + 0; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} \u22c5 2; = e^{2x}\u2713]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (d)/(dx)((1)/(2)e^{2x} + C)|= (1)/(2) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(e^{2x}) + (d)/(dx)(C); = (1)/(2) \u22c5 e^{2x} \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x) + 0; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} \u22c5 2; = e^{2x}\u2713]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nWe know how to integrate [MATH: e^x,] but how do we integrate a function like [MATH: e^{2x}?]\n\nContent:\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let [MATH: u=2x,] then\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.]\n\nContent:\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable [MATH: u,] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222be^{2x}dx|= \u222be^{u} \u22c5 (1)/(2)du; = (1)/(2)\u222be^{u}du; = (1)/(2)e^{u} + C; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} + C.]\n\nContent:\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (d)/(dx)((1)/(2)e^{2x} + C)|= (1)/(2) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(e^{2x}) + (d)/(dx)(C); = (1)/(2) \u22c5 e^{2x} \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x) + 0; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} \u22c5 2; = e^{2x}\u2713]"
      },
