    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe know how to integrate [MATH: e^x,] but how do we integrate a function like [MATH: e^{2x}?]\n\nContent:\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let [MATH: u=2x,] then\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.]\n\nContent:\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable [MATH: u,] as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222be^{2x}dx|= \u222be^{u} \u22c5 (1)/(2)du; = (1)/(2)\u222be^{u}du; = (1)/(2)e^{u} + C; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} + C.]\n\nContent:\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (d)/(dx)((1)/(2)e^{2x} + C)|= (1)/(2) \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(e^{2x}) + (d)/(dx)(C); = (1)/(2) \u22c5 e^{2x} \u22c5 (d)/(dx)(2x) + 0; = (1)/(2)e^{2x} \u22c5 2; = e^{2x}\u2713]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle{\\int e^{1-3x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIf we let [MATH: u=1-3x,] then\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.]\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of [MATH: u] and evaluate:\n\n[MATH: \u222be^{1 \u2212 3x}dx|= \u222be^{u} \u22c5 (\u2212 (1)/(3))du; = \u2212 (1)/(3)\u222be^{u}du; = \u2212 (1)/(3)e^{u} + C; = \u2212 (1)/(3)e^{1 \u2212 3x} + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b3e^{\u2212 6x}dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 e^{\u2212 6x} + C]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(2)e^{\u2212 6x} + C]\nc. [MATH: e^{\u2212 6x} + C]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 6e^{\u2212 6x} + C]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(2)e^{\u2212 6x} + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{3}(1)/(3)e^{x/3}dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: e \u2212 1]\nb. [MATH: e]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(e)]\nd. [MATH: 3]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle{\\int 3^{5+4x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIf we let [MATH: u=5+4x,] then\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 4 \\textrm d u.]\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of [MATH: u] and evaluate:\n\n[MATH: \u222b3^{5 + 4x}dx|= \u222b3^{u} \u22c5 (1)/(4)du; = (1)/(4)\u222b3^{u}du; = (1)/(4) \u22c5 (3^{u})/(\\ln 3) + C; = (3^{5 + 4x})/(4\\ln 3) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b3^{\u2212 x \u2212 6}dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (3^{x + 6})/(\\ln 3) + C]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (3^{\u2212 x \u2212 6})/(\\ln 3) + C]\nc. [MATH: (3^{x + 6})/(3) + C]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (3^{\u2212 x})/(\\ln 3) + C]\ne. [MATH: (3^{\u2212 x \u2212 6})/(\\ln 3) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b2^{6x \u2212 1}dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2^{6x \u2212 1})/(6) + C]\nb. [MATH: (2^{6x \u2212 1})/(6\\ln 2) + C]\nc. [MATH: (2^{3x \u2212 1})/(\\ln 2) + C]\nd. [MATH: (2^{6x \u2212 1})/(\\ln 2) + C]\ne. [MATH: (2^{3x \u2212 1})/(6) + C]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Tutorial: General Formulas for Integrating Exponential Functions With Linear Exponents\n\nContent:\nIf you're comfortable with memorizing formulas, then it's worth noting that\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222be^{px + q}dx|= (1)/(p)e^{px + q} + C,; \u222ba^{px + q}dx|= (1)/(p\\ln a)a^{px + q} + C.]\n\nContent:\nIf you don't like to memorize formulas, you can always use substitution to solve these types of integrals."
